      {
        "id": "p50-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Use symmetry of the normal distribution to explain why, for $a>0$, $P(Z<-a)=1-P(Z\\le a)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The normal curve is symmetric about $0$, so the area left of $-a$ equals the area right of $a$. Thus $P(Z<-a)=1-P(Z\\le a)$.",
          "worked": "The bell curve is symmetric about $z=0$, so the area to the left of $-a$ equals the area to the right of $a$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-a)&=P(Z>a)\\\\&=1-P(Z\\le a).\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-q9-b",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Use this to find the following probabilities. Give answers to 4 decimal places.",
        "layout": "grid",
        "columns": 4,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p50-q9-b-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "$P(Z<-1.2)$",
